INSERT INTO `ha_term_relationships` (`object_id`,`term_taxonomy_id`,`term_order`) SELECT @translation_id,tt.term_taxonomy_id,0 FROM `ha_term_taxonomy` tt JOIN `ha_terms` t ON t.term_id=tt.term_id WHERE @translation_id IS NOT NULL AND tt.taxonomy='category' AND t.slug='iraq' AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM `ha_term_relationships` r WHERE r.object_id=@translation_id AND r.term_taxonomy_id=tt.term_taxonomy_id) LIMIT 1;
INSERT INTO `ha_posts` (`post_author`,`post_date`,`post_date_gmt`,`post_content`,`post_title`,`post_excerpt`,`post_status`,`comment_status`,`ping_status`,`post_name`,`post_modified`,`post_modified_gmt`,`post_parent`,`guid`,`menu_order`,`post_type`,`post_mime_type`,`comment_count`) SELECT 1,NOW(),UTC_TIMESTAMP(),'<h2>Introduction: Why Greenhouse Irrigation Must Be Smart</h2>\n<p>Greenhouse cultivation, due to controlled environmental conditions, requires precise management of water resources. <strong>Greenhouse irrigation</strong> is no longer a simple process, but a precise engineering discipline that directly affects quality, root health, and ultimately yield. Unlike open-field crops, any irregularity in water distribution in greenhouses can lead to localized dryness, physiological stress, or root diseases. This section of the guide examines how selecting the appropriate system can reduce operational costs and maximize efficiency.</p>\n<p>The two main systems used in many greenhouse crops are drip and tape systems. While drip is suitable for trees and perennial covers with wider spacing, tape is a better option for annual crops with row planting (such as vegetables, cucurbits, and ornamental flowers). Understanding these fundamental distinctions is the first step in designing a sustainable and efficient system.</p>\n\n<h2>Technical Differences Between Tape and Drip in the Greenhouse Environment</h2>\n<p>The choice between tape and drip systems depends directly on the type of crop. Drip tape (Drip Tape) is usually thinner (2 to 3 mm) with a shorter useful life (one growing season). This characteristic makes its initial cost lower, making it ideal for frequent annual crops. In contrast, round and tube drip systems are thicker (4 to 8 mm) and have a multi-year lifespan, chosen for berry and grape trees and crops that require system longevity.</p>\n<p>One key point in greenhouse management is pressure definition. Modern drip lines are now produced in double-layer or side-sealed formats, which improve flow stability. To ensure that your selected drip line meets the plant\'s root zone requirements, reviewing the benefits of side-sealed drip lines can enhance your technical understanding. These lines reduce clogging at the emitters and guarantee more uniform water distribution along the row.</p>\n\n<h3>Key points in greenhouse drip line installation</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Emitter spacing selection:</strong> Emitter spacing must be suitable for plant rooting. For tomatoes and peppers, emitters spaced 15 to 20 centimeters apart are generally recommended.</li>\n<li><strong>Drip line orientation:</strong> Always position the drip line so that emitters face the main root zone rather than the stem, to prevent stem wetness and the increased risk of rot.</li>\n<li><strong>Connections:</strong> Use secure connections and standard fittings; leaks at connection points in high-humidity greenhouses can be a major source of fungal growth.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>If you intend to determine the seed quantity required per hectare to calculate the number of rows and drip line length, you can refer to specialized resources. For example, for small-scale or intensive cropping, knowing the seed rate per hectare for bush beans helps you determine the exact length of the required drip tape based on plant spacing.</p>\n<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img src="https://navar-abyari.ir/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/navar-article-generated/راهنمای-جامع-آبیاری-گلخانهای-تصویر-شاخص.webp" alt="راهنمای جامع آبیاری گلخانه‌ای - تصویر 1"/><figcaption>Greenhouse Irrigation comprehensive guide</figcaption></figure>\n\n\n\n<h2>Managing Pressure and Flow: The Key to Uniform Distribution</h2>\n<p>In greenhouse systems, pressure drop along the main line (Mother Line) is a common challenge. If the input pressure to the system is low, the outlets at the end of the line will experience dryness. To solve this problem, pressure-compensated valves are used in distribution lines. These valves create mechanical resistance, keeping the flow constant and independent of the input pressure.</p>\n<p>Calculating the total system flow rate is crucial. The total flow rate is equal to the sum of the flow rates of the active outlets on a main line. If the pump flow rate is lower than this value, the system pressure will drop. For those new to this field, understanding the relationship between pipe diameter, path length, and drip tape diameter is highly important. We recommend that before purchasing, you request detailed technical specifications using the appropriate drip tape selection method, based on the greenhouse slope and the type of crop coverage.</p>\n\n<h3>Comparison Table of Moisture Requirements for Some Greenhouse Crops</h3>\n<table>\n  <tr>\n    <th>Crop Type</th>\n    <th>Rooting Depth</th>\n    <th>Number of daily irrigation events</th>\n    <th>Type of recommended system</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Tomato</td>\n    <td>60–100 cm</td>\n    <td>2 to 4 (short and frequent)</td>\n    <td>Side-by-side drip line</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Leafy vegetables</td>\n    <td>30–50 cm</td>\n    <td>3 to 5</td>\n    <td>Drip tape or drip irrigation</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Ornamental flowers</td>\n    <td>Varies by species</td>\n    <td>Frequent and short</td>\n    <td>Circular drip irrigation</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>\n\n<p>For specific crops such as celery, the irrigation pattern may differ. Celery is sensitive to high soil moisture, and a sudden drop in moisture causes cracking. Study <a href="https://navar-abyari.ir/celery-drip-tape-irrigation-guide/">Drip tape irrigation guide for celery</a> It can help you adjust the irrigation chains for this sensitive crop.</p>\n\n<h2>Practical tips for increasing yield and reducing losses</h2>\n<p>One common mistake in <strong>greenhouse irrigation</strong>is the use of outdated or unsuitable systems that result in water waste and nutrient loss. Modern systems with \'precision irrigation\' capabilities provide real-time moisture control.</p>\n<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img src="https://navar-abyari.ir/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/navar-article-generated/راهنمای-جامع-آبیاری-گلخانهای-کاربرد-عملی.webp" alt="راهنمای جامع آبیاری گلخانه‌ای - تصویر 2"/><figcaption>Greenhouse irrigation comprehensive guide</figcaption></figure>\n\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Benefiting from fertigation:</strong> Direct all fertilizers with the water flow to the root zone. This practice saves on fertilizer costs and improves nutrient uptake.</li>\n<li><strong>Soil moisture monitoring:</strong> Use appropriate filters (filter size 80 or 120) to prevent outlet clogging. Clogged outlets are the main enemy of drip tape.</li>\n<li><strong>Relative humidity control:</strong> In summer, a decrease in greenhouse relative humidity can cause evaporative stress. Increasing irrigation frequency and reducing the volume per irrigation event is an effective solution.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p>Initial system installation costs should be evaluated against crop yield increases and loss reductions. Although the price of drip tape per hectare varies depending on quality and brand, investing in a high-quality system guarantees a return on investment within one to two growing seasons. To better understand project economics, you can search for how much drip tape costs per hectare to get a more accurate financial estimate.</p>\n\n\n\n<h2>Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)</h2>\n<p><strong>1. Is drip tape reusable?</strong><br>Standard drip tape is designed for one growing season. Use in a second or third season leads to reduced flow rates and clogging due to wall thickening and degradation. For permanent crops, use permanent drip lines.</p>\n\n<p><strong>2. How to prevent clogging of drip line outlets?</strong><br>Using water filtration systems, appropriate filters, and regular flushing of reservoirs is essential. Additionally, fertilizer must be injected from a separate reservoir at a point where the water flow is rapid to prevent sedimentation.</p>\n\n<p><strong>3. Is drip irrigation suitable for growing forage corn in a greenhouse?</strong><br>Forage corn is typically grown in open fields; however, if used in a controlled environment, the spacing between outlets must align with the planting distance. You can review further technical details regarding forage corn drip irrigation, although corn cultivation is less common in Iranian greenhouses.</p>\n<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img src="https://navar-abyari.ir/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/navar-article-generated/راهنمای-جامع-آبیاری-گلخانهای-جزئیات-فنی.webp" alt="راهنمای جامع آبیاری گلخانه‌ای - تصویر 3"/><figcaption>Greenhouse irrigation comprehensive guide</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h2>Conclusion: A step toward sustainable greenhouses</h2>\n<p>Development <strong>Greenhouse irrigation</strong> Moving toward smart and precision systems is not just a technical choice but an economic and environmental necessity. By understanding the differences between tape and drip lines, monitoring system pressure, and following practical maintenance guidelines, you can enhance crop yield.</p>\n<p>The final recommendation is to start with a thorough site and soil inspection before implementing any system. Understanding soil type, slope, and crop cover is the key to correct selection. Studying the importance of site inspection for tape and polyethylene pipes can protect you from costly errors during installation. With precise planning, your greenhouse will deliver not only better produce but also a healthier environment and higher efficiency.</p>\n<p><strong>Related articles:</strong> <a href="https://navar-abyari.ir/%d8%a7%d9%87%d9%85%db%8c%d8%aa-%d8%a8%d8%a7%d8%b2%d8%b1%d8%b3%db%8c-%d8%b2%d9%85%db%8c%d9%86-%d9%86%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b1-%d8%aa%db%8c%d9%be-%d9%88-%d9%84%d9%88%d9%84%d9%87/">The importance of site inspection for tape and polyethylene pipes</a>, <a href="https://navar-abyari.ir/%d9%81%d8%a7%d8%b5%d9%84%d9%87-%d9%86%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b1-%d8%aa%db%8c%d9%be-%d8%b0%d8%b1%d8%aa-%d8%b9%d9%84%d9%88%d9%81%d9%87-%d8%a7%db%8c-%da%a9%d8%a7%d8%b4%d8%aa-%d9%87%da%a9%d8%aa%d8%a7%d8%b1/">Tape line spacing for corn: Everything about forage corn</a>, <a href="https://navar-abyari.ir/%D9%85%D9%82%D8%AF%D8%A7%D8%B1-%D8%A8%D8%B0%D8%B1-%D8%B9%D8%AF%D8%B3-%D8%AF%D8%B1-%D9%87%DA%A9%D8%AA%D8%A7%D8%B1/">D985D982D8AFD8A7D8B1 D8A8D8B0D8B1 D8B9D8AFD8B3 D8AFD8B1 D987DAA9D8AAD8A7D8B1</a></p>','Comprehensive Guide to Greenhouse Irrigation: Principles, Selection, and Optimization of Drip and Trailing Tap Systems','Introduction to the best irrigation methods in closed environments, analysis of the differences between drip and line-tip systems, and practical solutions for reducing water consumption and improving greenhouse crop quality.','publish','closed','closed','greenhouse-irrigation-guide-drip-trailing-tap-optimization',NOW(),UTC_TIMESTAMP(),0,'',0,'post','',0 WHERE @source_post_id IS NOT NULL AND @translation_id IS NULL AND @slug_conflict IS NULL;
